# .env.bws.template — the target .env shape once Bitwarden Secrets Manager is live.
#
# The point: .env shrinks to ONE bootstrap secret (BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN) plus a
# few local-only conveniences. Everything sensitive (DATABASE_URL with the
# Neon password, API_KEY, future Splunk credentials) lives in BWS under the
# names below, and `scripts/dev` injects them at launch via `bws run`.
#
# Workflow (see scripts/dev and scripts/pull-secrets):
#   1. Put your access token in .env:      BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN=0.xxxx...
#   2. Regenerate .env from BWS anytime:   scripts/pull-secrets
#      (wiped .env? one command restores it — that's the whole point)
#   3. Launch with injection:              scripts/dev
#      (auto-detects BWS mode; falls back to plain .env values offline)
#
# ─── In BWS (secret name = env var name; create these in the SOC project) ──
#
# DATABASE_URL          the shared Neon URL (postgresql://...sslmode=require)
#                       or a local Postgres URL for dev-only secrets
# API_KEY               gate key for dangerous routes (blank/unset = gate off)
# OLLAMA_URL            e.g. http://127.0.0.1:11434 (local-only, still fine in BWS)
# CORS_ORIGINS          e.g. https://soc-plaform-livid.vercel.app
#
# Anything else the platform later reads from the environment (Splunk
# credentials when Phase 3 lands, etc.) goes here too — same naming rule.

# ─── Local-only (stays in .env, preserved by scripts/pull-secrets) ─────────
#
# BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN=0.xxxx.yyy...   ← the ONE bootstrap secret; never commit, never paste in chat
# SOC_PLATFORM_ROOT=/Users/you/soc-platform
# ENABLE_DOCS=1                    ← keep Swagger UI locally (prod never sets it)
# SOC_PORT=8000                    ← optional launcher port override

# ── What NOT to put back in .env once BWS is live ──
# Real passwords, connection strings, API keys. If .env holds more than
# BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN + the four local-only keys above, move the rest to BWS.
